          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delhi_Sultanate"
        }
      ],
      "tag_prose": "turkish_military_dominance"
    },
    {
      "tag": "dynastic_consolidation",
      "layer": "external_political",
      "description": "The Delhi Sultanate inherited the Ghurid administrative apparatus including experienced Persian scribes, Arab traders, and Afghan soldiers who had already pacified northern India",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q229411",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q229411"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Delhi Sultanate",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delhi_Sultanate"
        }
      ],
      "tag_prose": "ghurid_inheritance"
    },
    {
      "tag": "mongol_repulsion",
      "layer": "military_security",
      "description": "Unlike Persia and Central Asia, the Delhi Sultanate successfully repelled repeated Mongol invasions (1241–1306), positioning itself as the shield of Islam in South Asia and attracting Muslim refugees from Mongol-devastated lands",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q229411",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q229411"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Delhi Sultanate",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delhi_Sultanate"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "resource_concentration",
      "layer": "economic_commercial",
      "description": "Control of the fertile Gangetic Plain provided the agricultural surplus and population base that funded the sultanate's military campaigns and building program",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q229411",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q229411"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Delhi Sultanate",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delhi_Sultanate"
        }
      ],
      "tag_prose": "ganges_plain_agriculture"
    }
  ],
  "decline_factors": [
    {
      "tag": "external_military_pressure",
      "layer": "military_security",
      "description": "Timur's sack of Delhi (1398) killed an estimated 100,000 people in a single day; the survivors fled and the city was depopulated for years; the Tughluq dynasty never recovered",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q229411",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q229411"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Delhi Sultanate",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delhi_Sultanate"
        }
      ],
      "tag_prose": "timur_invasion"
    },
    {
      "tag": "tughluq_policy_failures",
      "layer": "economic_commercial",
      "description": "Muhammad bin Tughluq's forced transfer of the capital to Daulatabad caused mass deaths; his experimental token currency collapsed, wiping out merchants' savings; these disasters triggered province-by-province revolts",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q229411",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q229411"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Delhi Sultanate",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delhi_Sultanate"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "regional_revolts",
      "layer": "governance_institutional",
      "description": "Bengal, Gujarat, the Deccan, Jaunpur, Malwa, and Punjab all broke away as independent sultanates during the 14th–15th centuries, reducing Delhi to one of many regional powers",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q229411",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q229411"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Delhi Sultanate",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delhi_Sultanate"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "tag": "artillery_disadvantage",
      "layer": "military_security",
      "description": "The Lodi Sultanate lacked the gunpowder artillery that Babur brought from Central Asia; the First Battle of Panipat (1526) demonstrated a decisive technology gap",
      "sources": [
